# =============================================================================
# _redirects — Cloudflare Pages redirect rules
# Portfolio demo by 1MR LLC. Not a real financial services firm.
# =============================================================================
#
# FORMAT
# ------
#   /source-path   /destination-path   [status]
#
# One rule per line. Whitespace-separated. Lines beginning with # are
# comments. Status defaults to 302 if omitted — always state it explicitly,
# because the default is almost never what you want for a site migration.
#
# 301 vs 302 — this is the decision that matters
# ----------------------------------------------
#   301 Moved Permanently
#     Tells search engines the old URL is dead and its accumulated ranking
#     signal should transfer to the destination. Use this for a site
#     migration: it is how you keep the SEO equity a legacy site spent years
#     earning. Browsers cache 301s aggressively and, on some versions,
#     effectively forever — so a wrong 301 is expensive to walk back. Get the
#     destination right before you ship it.
#
#   302 Found (temporary)
#     Tells search engines the old URL is still canonical and will return.
#     Ranking signal stays with the source. Use for A/B tests, seasonal
#     landing pages, maintenance windows, geo-routing — anything you intend
#     to undo.
#
#   Rule of thumb: if you will never serve the old URL again, 301. If you
#   might, 302. When genuinely unsure, ship 302 first — a 302 can be promoted
#   to a 301 later, but a cached 301 cannot easily be revoked.
#
# CLOUDFLARE PAGES SPECIFICS
# --------------------------
#   * Static files win. If a real file exists at the source path, it is
#     served and the redirect never fires. Every source path below is
#     verified disjoint from the 22 real pages by tools/verify.sh.
#   * Rules are evaluated top to bottom; the first match wins. Order the
#     specific above the general.
#   * Query strings on the SOURCE are ignored for matching but are forwarded
#     to the destination automatically. Query strings on the DESTINATION are
#     preserved as written, which is what the three vanity rules rely on.
#   * Limits: 2,100 static rules per project, of which up to 100 may be
#     dynamic (containing :placeholders or * splats). We use 18.
#   * This file must sit in the root of the build output directory. With no
#     build step, that is the repo root — which is where it is.
#
# VERIFYING
# ---------
#   curl -sI https://<your-domain>/about-us | head -n 2
#   Expect: HTTP/2 301  and  location: /about
#
#   Follow the whole chain and assert a single hop:
#   curl -sIL -o /dev/null -w '%{num_redirects} %{url_effective}\n' \
#     https://<your-domain>/about-us
#
#   tools/verify.sh --live <base-url> checks all 18 in one pass.
#
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VANITY LINKS — one per loan officer
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These are the URLs an agent actually types into a text message, so they are
# short, lowercase and unambiguous when read aloud. Each carries the officer
# slug through as a query parameter that /start reads and resolves against
# data/loan-officers.json.
#
# 301 rather than 302: these short links are permanent public identifiers and
# we want them consolidated onto /start for search purposes.

/sarah      /start?lo=sarah      301
/marcus     /start?lo=marcus     301
/jordan     /start?lo=jordan     301


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LEGACY URL MIGRATION — 15 rules
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The shape of a real migration: paths from a previous WordPress or hand-built
# site, mapped to their nearest equivalent on the new one. All 301, because
# the old site is being retired and its ranking signal should follow.
#
# Note the mapping principle — every legacy URL points at the closest
# equivalent page, never at the homepage as a catch-all. Bulk-redirecting a
# retired site to "/" is the single most common migration mistake: Google
# treats a redirect to an irrelevant page as a soft 404 and discards the
# ranking signal entirely, which defeats the reason for redirecting at all.

# Home and index variants
/home                     /                 301
/index.php                /                 301

# Company pages
/about-us                 /about            301
/our-team                 /team             301
/our-team/sarah-chen      /team/sarah       301
/our-team/marcus-reyes    /team/marcus      301
/our-team/jordan-ellis    /team/jordan      301

# Conversion paths — the old site scattered these across three URLs
/services                 /apply            301
/apply-now                /apply            301
/loan-application         /apply            301

# Contact and partner pages
/contact-us               /contact          301
/realtors                 /agents           301
/schedule-a-call          /calendar         301

# Content
/testimonials             /reviews          301
/news                     /blog             301


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PATTERNS NOT USED HERE, DOCUMENTED FOR REFERENCE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Splat — captures the remainder of a path into :splat
#   /blog/2023/*   /blog/:splat   301
#
# Named placeholder — captures one path segment
#   /lo/:name      /start?lo=:name   301
#
# Both count against the 100-rule dynamic limit rather than the 2,100 static
# limit, so prefer explicit static rules while the list is small and legible.
# A dynamic rule is the right call once you are migrating hundreds of URLs
# that share a predictable shape.
